                        ],
                        "form": "stickled"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "cb31dfb4-6df3-4045-aa1a-20bf06db5177",
                        "entry_id": "e0bf5676-b0f4-3d22-a642-75d04d2b3371",
                        "tags": [
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "stickled"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ea49fa62-4c09-3d31-9927-3c3d2ead4483",
                        "entry_id": "e0bf5676-b0f4-3d22-a642-75d04d2b3371",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To act as referee or arbiter; to mediate."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "British",
                            "dialectal",
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6a685b2d-ec52-30a4-b69d-b5d6dc54d941",
                        "entry_id": "e0bf5676-b0f4-3d22-a642-75d04d2b3371",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To argue or struggle for."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "British",
                            "archaic",
                            "dialectal"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "484653cc-85eb-4d2e-975e-fe2ee46f95f6",
                                "sense_id": "6a685b2d-ec52-30a4-b69d-b5d6dc54d941",
                                "text": "‘She has other people than poor little you to think about, and has gone abroad with them; so you needn’t be in the least afraid she’ll stickle this time for her rights.’"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "8d7a7c33-1c0b-3a59-8b75-e1b91a9c318b",
                        "entry_id": "e0bf5676-b0f4-3d22-a642-75d04d2b3371",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To raise objections; to argue stubbornly, especially over minor or trivial matters."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "British",
                            "dialectal"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fd7a2fa1-64fc-4791-8ac7-7213faa60945",
                                "sense_id": "8d7a7c33-1c0b-3a59-8b75-e1b91a9c318b",
                                "text": "Miserable new Berline! Why could not Royalty go in some old Berline similar to that of other men? Flying for life, one does not stickle about his vehicle."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0b5162fd-d5f5-39a6-8c48-c54c91cb9a5b",
                        "entry_id": "e0bf5676-b0f4-3d22-a642-75d04d2b3371",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To separate, as combatants; hence, to quiet, to appease, as disputants."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "British",
                            "dialectal",
                            "obsolete",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c72b8fbe-ae0c-41fe-a87f-fb1815db9a7f",
                                "sense_id": "0b5162fd-d5f5-39a6-8c48-c54c91cb9a5b",
                                "text": "Which [question] violently they pursue, / Nor stickled would they be."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9d874e69-45cd-3b59-96b4-39d793730595",
                        "entry_id": "e0bf5676-b0f4-3d22-a642-75d04d2b3371",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To intervene in; to stop, or put an end to, by intervening."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "British",
                            "dialectal",
                            "obsolete",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "68f0fca9-6ab8-43c8-90c0-0d46d75b1ed9",
                                "sense_id": "9d874e69-45cd-3b59-96b4-39d793730595",
                                "text": "They ran to him, and, pulling him back by force, stickled that unnatural fray.",
                                "translation": "The New Arcadia",
                                "english": "The New Arcadia"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4f4b0e1e-60cc-3e87-80c7-9c1c2b751d9d",
                        "entry_id": "e0bf5676-b0f4-3d22-a642-75d04d2b3371",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To separate combatants by intervening."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "British",
                            "dialectal",
                            "intransitive",
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "65f9d1ef-710f-4130-ac18-f65f03e05db1",
                                "sense_id": "4f4b0e1e-60cc-3e87-80c7-9c1c2b751d9d",
                                "text": "When he [the angel] sees half of the Christians are already killed, and all the rest in a fair way to be routed, [he]stickles betwixt the remainders of God’s host, and the race of fiends."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a7405c7c-67a0-3207-8f36-4b360e6b5760",
                        "entry_id": "e0bf5676-b0f4-3d22-a642-75d04d2b3371",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To contend, contest, or altercate, especially in a pertinacious manner on insufficient grounds."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "British",
                            "dialectal",
                            "intransitive",
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "53fdcd94-42a0-49a3-8a27-a9bdc385be0d",
                                "sense_id": "a7405c7c-67a0-3207-8f36-4b360e6b5760",
                                "text": "Fortune, as she’s wont, turned fickle, / And for the foe began to stickle."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f5cef587-5051-471c-aeb5-071fe4ab85f4",
                                "sense_id": "a7405c7c-67a0-3207-8f36-4b360e6b5760",
                                "text": "for paltry punk they roar and stickle"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1bbca1f2-b3e9-4f76-9307-fec59f99d6a1",
                                "sense_id": "a7405c7c-67a0-3207-8f36-4b360e6b5760",
                                "text": "the obstinacy with which he stickles for the wrong"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
